From: Gemini PDA Linux bring-up
Subject: [PATCH] clk: do not gate the five clocks MT6797 is using but nobody holds

WORKAROUND, NOT A CURE, AND DELIBERATELY A NAMED LIST. #85.

MT6797 loses roughly half its reboots to a 5-20 minute loop. Root cause, captured
through the ramoops console zone (which survives this SoC's reset, so a dead boot
is readable on the next live one):

  [    1.010716] clk-unused: gating mm_dsi1_interface_clock
  [    1.011373] clk-unused: gating mm_dsi0_interface_clock
  [    1.012031] clk-unused: gating audio_h_sel
  [    1.012557] clk-unused: gating aud_2_sel
  [    1.013062] clk-unused: gating aud_1_sel
  [   24.025716] watchdog: Watchdog detected hard LOCKUP on cpu 3

Five clocks of 111. A healthy boot prints its next line ONE MILLISECOND after
"clk: Disabling unused clocks"; this one printed nothing for twenty-three seconds
and then lost two CPUs. Gate a clock a block silently depends on and the next
MMIO to it never returns. /init never runs, systemd never claims /dev/watchdog,
and watchdog.open_timeout=120 reclaims the machine -- measured five times at
118-120 s.

WHY A LIST AND NOT clk_ignore_unused. That flag leaves ALL 111 unused clocks
running, on a device with no CPU thermal sensor that browns out 71 seconds after
being unplugged (#59, B-47). Five clocks is a cost worth paying; 111 is not. It
also documents exactly which clocks this SoC cannot survive losing, which
clk_ignore_unused hides.

WHY NOT JUST FIX THE DRIVERS -- WE TRIED, AND IT WAS NOT ENOUGH.
patches/v6.6/sound/0001 makes the audio node claim CLK_TOP_MUX_AUD_1/_AUD_2/
_AUDIO_H (which mt8183 and mt7622 both claim and MT6797 did not) and holds them
with devm_clk_get_enabled(). That is correct and it is still in the series --
but build #117 carried it and STILL LOOPED. Which taught the thing worth
carrying:

  THE LAST CLOCK NAMED IN THE RECORD IS WHERE OUTPUT STOPPED, NOT NECESSARILY
  WHAT STOPPED IT.

The diagnostic prints the name BEFORE the disable, so a CPU that hard-locks on
an MMIO after an EARLIER gate truncates the log a few lines later. aud_1_sel was
simply the last line to make it out. The display pair gated immediately before is
at least as likely, on a device whose panel and touch share a power domain and
which already has a blocker (B-36) about touching DSI registers with the display
asleep.

So this covers all five while the individual owners are sorted out. Removing an
entry is a one-line experiment that says whether that clock mattered.

Evidence: 04-docs/captures/85-boot-loop-20260828/.

--- a/drivers/clk/clk.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/clk.c
@@ -1399,6 +1399,39 @@
 		goto unlock_out;
 
 	/*
+	 * #85: MT6797 has clocks no driver holds enabled that the hardware is
+	 * using anyway. Gating one wedges the boot: the CPU that next touches
+	 * the affected block hard-locks, /init never runs, systemd never claims
+	 * /dev/watchdog, and watchdog.open_timeout=120 reclaims the machine --
+	 * measured five times at 118-120 s. A captured failing boot gets five
+	 * clocks into the sweep and dies; these are those five, in the order
+	 * they were gated.
+	 *
+	 * Deliberately a NAMED list rather than clk_ignore_unused, which leaves
+	 * all 111 unused clocks running on a device that browns out 71 seconds
+	 * after being unplugged. Five is a cost worth paying; 111 is not.
+	 *
+	 * Still a workaround. The right fix is for the owning drivers to hold
+	 * what they use. patches/v6.6/sound/0001 does that for the audio group
+	 * and was NOT sufficient alone -- which is what narrowed the suspects
+	 * to the display pair. See 04-docs/captures/85-boot-loop-20260828/.
+	 */
+	if (core->name) {
+		static const char * const gemini_keep[] = {
+			"mm_dsi1_interface_clock",
+			"mm_dsi0_interface_clock",
+			"audio_h_sel",
+			"aud_2_sel",
+			"aud_1_sel",
+		};
+		int k;
+
+		for (k = 0; k < ARRAY_SIZE(gemini_keep); k++)
+			if (!strcmp(core->name, gemini_keep[k]))
+				goto unlock_out;
+	}
+
+	/*
 	 * some gate clocks have special needs during the disable-unused
 	 * sequence.  call .disable_unused if available, otherwise fall
 	 * back to .disable
